      "source_id": "1",
      "stem": "A probe is located relative to a space station at the position vector \\(\\vec r = -8\\hat i + 15\\hat j\\), where distances are measured in kilometers. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The distance between the probe and the space station is 23 km.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This comes from adding the component magnitudes, \\(8+15\\), instead of finding the vector magnitude. Distance from the origin must be computed using \\(|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\), not by simple addition of components."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The distance between the probe and the space station is 17 km.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The probe's distance from the station is the magnitude of its position vector. Using \\(|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{(-8)^2+15^2}=\\sqrt{64+225}=\\sqrt{289}=17\\,\\text{km}\\), we find the correct distance is \\(17\\,\\text{km}\\)."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The distance between the probe and the space station is 15 km.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This uses only the \\(y\\)-component and ignores the horizontal displacement. A single component is not the same as the full distance from the origin."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The distance between the probe and the space station is 8 km.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This uses only the \\(x\\)-component magnitude and neglects the \\(y\\)-component. That is a component-versus-magnitude error."
        }
      ],
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "skipped": false,
